  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Monday)\nIssued at 1236 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nAn upper ridge axis currently sits over the Rockies and Desert\nSouthwest with northwest flow aloft across the Midwest down to\nthe Gulf. Normally this pattern would bring a cold front down to\ncool the region, but instead we have been faced with a multi-day\nheat wave. With no front or meaningful cloud cover expected in\nthe next 24 hours, dangerous heat is forecast to continue Tuesday\nwith heat index values in excess of 110 F across the entire Mid-\nSouth. A few showers could occur tonight in association with\ndecaying convection from the north, but any rain will be\nnegligible in both amounts and impacts to Tuesday's heat.\n\nBy Tuesday evening, an upper wave, produced by overnight\nconvection across the High Plains, will propagate into the\nOzarks. CAMs go on to produce a few showers as this feature moves\ninto the region through the early evening. As the night\nprogresses, CAMs are in general agreement that convective\ncoverage will pick up with locally heavy rainfall along an axis\nextending from northern Arkansas into north-central Mississippi.\nAs of right now, rainfall totals look to only approach 1.0\" in\nmost areas with locally heavier totals possible under stronger\nareas of rainfall. Fortunately for us, this will mark the\nbeginning of a cooldown that is expected to last the rest of the\nforecast period.\n\nBehind the weak wave Tuesday night and into Wednesday morning,\nthe synoptic troughing across the eastern CONUS will finally drag\ndown more northerly flow. This will act to help the area of\nsurface high pressure to our north move far enough south to bring\ncooler air into the region. High temperatures will drop to around\n90 F. Heat index values will drop to around or below 100 F in\nmost areas, aside from the Mississippi River Delta in Arkansas\nand Mississippi where heat index values may still reach 105+.\nBeyond Wednesday, this pattern looks likely to hold with the\nupper ridge over the western CONUS retrograding further west with\nthe axis of stronger northwesterly flow moving westward along\nwith it.\n\nGiven the cooler air that will settle in over the region, a\nbaroclinic zone will form along a stationary boundary through the\nMid-South, albeit relatively weak. Multiple medium- to long-range\nmodels show a lee surface cyclone propagating eastward through\nthe Middle Mississippi River Valley, bringing another shot of\nrain Friday and into Saturday. Some areas could see locally heavy\nrainfall associated with diurnally driven convection. However,\nthe exact extent of the heavier rainfall totals will be\ndetermined in the coming days as forecast uncertainties in the\ntiming, strength, and position of the surface low become better\nresolved. As of right now, 1.0\" - 1.5\" are most likely with\nheavier amounts confined near the Tennessee River Valley.\n\nA cold front associated with the surface cyclone will pass by\nSunday morning, ushering another round of cooler air and dry\nweather. Therefore, the cooling trend is expected to continue\nbeyond this weekend and into next week. Many models keep residual\nupper troughing over the southern Mississippi River Valley early\nnext week through the end of the period, marking a signal that\nthe extent of the cooler temperatures may reach further into next\nweek outside of the forecast period.",
